import { reading } from "./sources.js";

export const MONTH_ONE = [
  reading(1, "correction", "Obedience", "After the Amen Comes the Answer",
    "Turning one heard instruction into a concrete response", "James 1:22",
    "A service can move our emotions while leaving our habits untouched. The question after the final amen is not only what we enjoyed, but what we will now obey.",
    `James addresses people who hear the Word and yet deceive themselves. Their problem is not a shortage of information. They mistake receiving instruction for responding to it. A mirror can reveal a stain, but looking into it does not wash the face. In the same way, a sermon exposes a choice that must be made outside the meeting.

JCTM's Correction Mandate calls believers back to the authority of Scripture. That return becomes personal when we stop applying every correction to somebody else. Perhaps the passage requires an overdue payment, an abandoned lie, or a different way of treating a colleague. The next faithful step may be quiet and costly rather than impressive.

Obedience does not purchase salvation; it expresses a heart being changed by grace. Ask Christ for strength to respond, and make the response specific enough to recognize. Spiritual excitement fades, but an instruction practiced becomes part of a transformed life. Let the message travel home with you and reach the decision that has remained unchanged.`,
    "Write down one instruction from your most recent Bible reading. Identify a concrete action, complete it before bedtime, and tell a trusted believer what you learned through obeying it.",
    "Father, rescue me from enjoying truth without practicing it. Give me courage to respond to the instruction You have already made clear, through Jesus Christ. Amen.",
    "By Christ's grace, I will act on the Word I have heard."),
  reading(2, "holiness", "Holiness", "Begin with Your Own Mirror",
    "Examining personal faults before correcting a neighbour", "Matthew 7:5",
    "It is easier to recognize another person's fault than to admit our own. Jesus brings the work of correction close to home before allowing it to reach our neighbour.",
    `Jesus does not forbid all moral discernment. He exposes the hypocrisy of attempting delicate work on another person's eye while a beam obstructs our own vision. The instruction is to remove our own obstruction first, so that help for another person can become clear-sighted rather than self-righteous.

This matters in a ministry that values correction. A truthful warning can still be delivered from an untruthful position. If I condemn a neighbour's dishonesty while hiding my own manipulation, my criticism becomes a shelter from repentance. Christ's standard must examine the person speaking as well as the person being addressed.

Self-examination is not an excuse to ignore harm or refuse necessary accountability. It changes our posture. We confess what is ours, seek appropriate help, and approach others as people who also need mercy. The aim is restoration rather than the pleasure of being superior. Before preparing the speech that will expose someone else, let the Lord show you the obedience you have been postponing in yourself.`,
    "Before a difficult correction, name your own contribution to the situation. Confess it without qualification. Then ask whether your proposed words seek restoration, safety, and truth rather than personal victory.",
    "Lord Jesus, uncover the faults I excuse in myself. Make my repentance honest and my correction humble, so that I can help others without pretending to be beyond mercy. Amen.",
    "I submit myself to Christ's correction before correcting another person."),
  reading(3, "primitive", "Spiritual Growth", "Bring the Message to the Scriptures",
    "Checking a teachers claims by daily examination of Scripture", "Acts 17:11",
    "Respect for a preacher does not require surrendering biblical examination. The Bereans received Paul's message eagerly, yet opened the Scriptures to discover whether the teaching was true.",
    `Luke commends the Bereans for combining readiness with investigation. Their openness was not gullibility, and their examination was not hostility. They listened carefully and searched the Scriptures daily. Even the preaching of an apostle was brought to the written witness of God's revelation.

JCTM's emphasis on Primitive Christianity invites this same seriousness. A title, a crowd, or a compelling story cannot make an interpretation correct. Read the passage being cited. Notice who is speaking, to whom the words are addressed, and how the argument develops. If a claim cannot survive its biblical context, admiration must not protect it from correction.

Examination also requires humility in the listener. We are not searching for a verse to defend an opinion we refuse to reconsider. We are asking the Word to correct teacher and hearer alike. Questions can be patient, respectful, and thorough. A mature faith neither swallows every confident claim nor dismisses every challenge. It learns to welcome truth because God's Word, rather than a personality, holds the final authority.`,
    "Choose one claim from a recent sermon. Read its main passage and the surrounding chapter. Record what the text actually supports and ask a respectful question where an explanation remains unclear.",
    "God of truth, give me an eager mind and an honest heart. Keep me from suspicion and gullibility, and teach me to examine every teaching under Your Word. Amen.",
    "Scripture will govern my convictions more than a speaker's reputation."),
  reading(4, "correction", "Christian Living", "When Custom Claims God's Authority",
    "Distinguishing human religious traditions from divine commands", "Mark 7:8",
    "A practice can feel sacred simply because it has been familiar for years. Jesus asks a harder question: does this custom serve God's command, or has it taken its place?",
    `In Mark 7, religious leaders scrutinize ceremonial habits while overlooking the commandments of God. Jesus points to a system in which a religious designation could be used to avoid caring for parents. What appeared devout had become a way of refusing a plain moral obligation.

Traditions are not automatically harmful. Familiar patterns can help a congregation worship and learn together. The danger comes when human arrangements are treated as God's unchangeable law, or when keeping a custom excuses disobedience. A special garment, ceremony, or vocabulary cannot cancel the duty to love, tell the truth, and care for those entrusted to us.

The Correction Mandate requires more than replacing someone else's customs with our own preferences. Every practice must remain accountable to Scripture. Ask what a tradition teaches, whom it burdens, and whether it displaces a clear command. Hold helpful customs gratefully, but do not bind another person's conscience where God has not spoken. Faithfulness belongs to the Lord, not to the fear of doing something differently from previous generations.`,
    "Identify one religious practice you have treated as compulsory. Find its biblical basis, distinguish command from preference, and make sure it does not become an excuse to neglect a clear responsibility.",
    "Father, free me from confusing familiarity with Your authority. Help me honor what is good in tradition while refusing any practice that sets aside Your commands. Amen.",
    "I will honor God's commands above inherited religious pressure."),
  reading(5, "primitive", "Spiritual Growth", "Receive the Counsel You Would Not Choose",
    "Welcoming uncomfortable parts of the biblical message", "Acts 20:27",
    "Most of us have favorite passages and subjects we quietly avoid. Paul reminds the Ephesian elders that faithful ministry does not hide the parts of God's counsel that might be unwelcome.",
    `Paul's farewell is marked by responsibility. He has not withheld the counsel of God from those he served. He has taught publicly and personally, called people to repentance and faith, and warned shepherds about dangers facing the church. Love did not permit selective silence.

Readers can also construct a selective gospel. We may welcome comfort but resist repentance, celebrate gifts but neglect character, or discuss future events while refusing present responsibilities. Scripture refuses to let one cherished emphasis swallow everything else God has revealed.

Receiving the whole counsel does not mean pretending to understand every difficult verse immediately. It means remaining teachable when a passage challenges our priorities. We can ask questions, examine context, and seek wise explanation without removing an uncomfortable text from our obedience. JCTM's call back to biblical Christianity requires this breadth. Let promises encourage you, warnings awaken you, commands direct you, and the gospel keep each in proper relationship. A healthy diet includes more than the sweetness we would naturally choose.`,
    "Read a biblical passage outside your usual favorites. Write one comfort, one correction, and one question it raises. Bring the question to a responsible teacher instead of avoiding the passage.",
    "Lord, enlarge my willingness to receive Your whole Word. Keep me from selecting only what pleases me, and form a balanced, faithful understanding of the gospel. Amen.",
    "I welcome God's correction as well as His comfort."),
  reading(6, "correction", "Love", "A Gentle Voice Can Carry Firm Truth",
    "Correcting doctrinal error without humiliating the learner", "2 Timothy 2:25",
    "Some conversations about doctrine become contests in which one person must lose face. Paul describes a different servant: someone who holds truth firmly while treating the mistaken person with meekness.",
    `Paul tells Timothy that the Lord's servant must not be quarrelsome. Gentleness is not a reduction in the seriousness of error. It is the manner suited to a person who knows that repentance is God's gift rather than the trophy of a superior debater.

Mockery may silence someone publicly without persuading them inwardly. Patient explanation makes room for honest questions and the possibility of change. We can identify a false claim, show why Scripture contradicts it, and refuse to attack a person's dignity. Where a teacher is exploiting people, firmness and protective action remain necessary; gentleness does not require concealing abuse.

The Correction Mandate is best served when the goal is recovery from error, not entertainment at another person's expense. Before entering a theological argument, ask whether you are willing to listen, explain, and pray for the person you disagree with. Truth does not need cruelty to become strong. A disciplined tongue can make the way of repentance clearer rather than turning it into a public humiliation.`,
    "Rewrite one correction you are tempted to deliver harshly. Remove insults, state the disputed claim accurately, and explain the relevant Scripture. Choose a private conversation when public protection is not required.",
    "Father, guard my tongue when truth is disputed. Give me patience without compromise and firmness without contempt, so that correction may lead toward understanding and repentance. Amen.",
    "I can defend biblical truth without despising the person before me."),
  reading(7, "holiness", "Obedience", "Do Not Measure the Mint and Miss Mercy",
    "Keeping justice and mercy central while practicing religious detail", "Matthew 23:23",
    "Careful religious habits can coexist with careless treatment of people. Jesus confronts leaders who count tiny offerings precisely while neglecting the weightier demands of justice, mercy, and faith.",
    `The rebuke in Matthew 23 does not praise sloppiness. Jesus exposes distorted proportion. His hearers can attend to a minute detail and still abandon something central. Precision becomes a hiding place when it allows us to feel obedient while those around us experience injustice.

Our own religious life can develop the same imbalance. We may arrive early, keep a strict program, and debate small differences while ignoring an employee's complaint or a vulnerable person's need. These activities are not wrong in themselves. They become dangerous when they supply a reputation for holiness that our relationships contradict.

Biblical righteousness reaches both worship and conduct. JCTM's emphasis on moral transformation asks us to examine what our devotion actually produces. Does it make us fairer, more merciful, and more faithful? Are we using detail to avoid a difficult act of justice? Let the important things remain important. The God who sees our offerings also sees how we treat the person who has no power to reward or defend us.`,
    "Review your religious commitments alongside your responsibilities to people. Correct one unfair arrangement or overlooked need this week. Keep useful disciplines, but refuse to let them excuse a lack of mercy.",
    "Lord Jesus, restore the right proportions in my obedience. Help me practice justice, mercy, and faith, and never use religious carefulness to conceal an unloving life. Amen.",
    "My worship will be accompanied by justice, mercy, and faithfulness."),
  reading(8, "authority", "Community", "Greatness That Does Not Dominate",
    "Rejecting domination as the measure of Christian leadership", "Mark 10:43",
    "Authority can be used to make others smaller or to help them grow. Jesus interrupts His disciples' ambition with a model of greatness that cannot be measured by domination.",
    `James and John seek places of honor, and the other disciples become indignant. Jesus contrasts the rulers who lord power over others with the life He requires among His followers. Greatness in His company is expressed through service.

This challenges leadership in a church, household, business, or public office. Rank does not grant permission to intimidate, silence legitimate questions, or demand personal loyalty above truth. A leader's authority is entrusted for a purpose beyond the leader's comfort. Those under that authority remain people made in God's image.

Service is not the abandonment of boundaries or responsibility. Good leaders make difficult decisions, confront wrongdoing, and protect the vulnerable. They do these things without turning their position into a claim of personal superiority. JCTM's warnings about abusive religious authority invite us to examine how our own influence feels to others. Does it equip people to stand faithfully before God, or train them to fear our displeasure? Christ makes room for leadership that is strong enough to serve rather than needing to dominate.`,
    "Ask someone affected by your decisions whether they can raise a concern safely. Listen without defending your rank. Change one leadership habit that protects your pride more than it serves their growth.",
    "Jesus, reshape my understanding of influence. Remove the desire to control people for my comfort, and teach me to lead with responsible strength and genuine service. Amen.",
    "The authority entrusted to me will serve people rather than dominate them."),
  reading(9, "authority", "Christian Living", "A Uniform Is Not an Exemption",
    "Refusing extortion and false accusation when entrusted with public power", "Luke 3:14",
    "John the Baptist did not give soldiers a separate moral code. Their power made obedience more urgent, not less important. Repentance had to reach the way they used their position.",
    `When soldiers ask what they should do, John addresses violence, false accusation, and discontent with wages. These are not abstract religious subjects. They concern the daily temptations of people who can frighten others into surrendering money, dignity, or safety.

The JCTM sermon description on this passage explicitly warns security officers against intimidation and false accusations. The principle also reaches anyone with leverage: a supervisor, gatekeeper, official, or influential relative. The ability to make life difficult for another person must never become a private source of income.

Contentment does not prohibit seeking fair wages or reporting unjust working conditions. It prohibits making innocent people pay for our frustration. Genuine repentance may require stopping an established practice, returning what was wrongly taken, and using lawful channels to address grievances. Religious participation cannot sanctify exploitation. Today, the question is not whether our position earns public respect, but whether our conduct respects the people who must deal with us. God sees the encounter that happens away from a supervisor's attention.`,
    "Examine any fee, favor, or threat connected with your position. Stop an improper demand, document legitimate charges clearly, and pursue accountability safely if your workplace expects wrongdoing.",
    "Righteous Father, keep me from exploiting anyone through power or influence. Give me courage to reject false accusation and extortion, and help me use my position justly. Amen.",
    "No position places me above God's call to justice."),
  reading(10, "authority", "Holiness", "Justice Must Not Recognize a Face",
    "Making fair judgments without favouring status or personal connections", "Deuteronomy 16:19",
    "A familiar name can change the way a case sounds to us. Scripture warns that justice becomes crooked when friendship, wealth, or reputation determines whose evidence receives a hearing.",
    `The command to Israel's judges forbids distorted judgment, partiality, and bribery. Justice requires more than a decision that benefits someone we like. It requires a process in which truth matters even when the result is inconvenient to our group.

We may not sit in a courtroom, yet we make judgments at work, at home, and in church. We decide whose complaint to believe, whose misconduct to excuse, and who must provide impossible proof. If an influential person's reputation is protected at the expense of a vulnerable person, loyalty has displaced righteousness.

Fairness does not mean believing every allegation without examination. It means listening carefully, applying consistent standards, preserving evidence, and allowing appropriate independent investigation. JCTM's insistence that authority remains under God's commandments applies to these ordinary decisions. Ask whether you would judge the facts differently if the names were exchanged. God does not need us to protect His honor through a dishonest verdict. Truthful justice is an act of faith in the One who sees without favoritism.`,
    "Before deciding a dispute, separate the evidence from your relationship with the people involved. Hear the person with less influence, and seek an impartial reviewer when your loyalty creates a conflict.",
    "God of justice, expose my partiality. Help me hear fairly, judge carefully, and refuse the convenience of protecting a familiar name at another person's expense. Amen.",
    "I will seek what is right rather than what protects my circle."),
  reading(11, "authority", "Stewardship", "The Wage That Must Not Wait",
    "Paying workers promptly instead of withholding earned wages", "James 5:4",
    "A delayed payment may feel like a bookkeeping matter to the person holding the money. To a worker's household, it can mean an unpaid rent, an empty kitchen, or a broken promise.",
    `James speaks with severity about wages fraudulently withheld from laborers. The workers' cries are not lost in the noise of a prosperous employer's life. They reach the Lord. Money can move from one account to another, but the moral obligation remains visible to God.

This warning reaches household staff, contractors, casual laborers, and employees as surely as workers in a field. Once a wage has been earned, it is not a gift we may distribute whenever we feel generous. Financial pressure should be communicated honestly rather than concealed behind excuses while less necessary spending continues.

Biblical stewardship includes the other person's need and agreed rights. A ministry or Christian business must not use spiritual language to make exploitation appear sacrificial. If a genuine dispute exists, address it promptly and fairly. If payment is due, pay it. Worship is not made more impressive by money kept from the people who helped produce it. Let the worker's dignity become part of the way you honor the God who hears their cry.`,
    "Check whether you owe wages or agreed fees. Pay what is due, or communicate a realistic written repayment arrangement without demanding silence or spiritualizing the worker's hardship.",
    "Lord, make me faithful in obligations to those who work for me. Forgive excuses that hide injustice, and help me handle their wages with urgency and respect. Amen.",
    "I will honor earned wages as an obligation, not a favor."),
  reading(12, "authority", "Holiness", "The Gift That Buys a Verdict",
    "Refusing benefits that corrupt impartial decisions", "Exodus 23:8",
    "Not every gift is innocent. A benefit offered near an important decision may quietly ask us to trade a clear conscience for gratitude to the giver.",
    `Exodus warns that a bribe blinds the discerning and twists the words of the righteous. The danger is not limited to people who openly intend to be corrupt. A person who once judged clearly can begin explaining away facts after accepting a benefit.

The temptation often arrives with respectable language: appreciation, facilitation, a token, or help for the family. The question is what the gift expects in return. If it buys access, silence, preference, or a distorted verdict, its label cannot make it righteous. Christian integrity requires examining the relationship between the benefit and our responsibility.

JCTM's teaching against manipulation and misuse of authority calls for transparent conduct rather than merely avoiding public scandal. Disclose a conflict, refuse an improper advantage, and follow clear procedures. Where refusing a bribe creates risk, seek appropriate support rather than navigating the pressure alone. A smaller income with a truthful decision is better than a reward that trains us to call wrong right. God can be honored without selling the trust others have placed in us.`,
    "Review a gift connected to a decision you control. Disclose it to the appropriate person, return an improper benefit, and create a clear boundary before another offer arrives.",
    "Father, protect my judgment from the influence of dishonest gifts. Give me courage to refuse advantages that would cost my integrity and harm those depending on a fair decision. Amen.",
    "My judgment will not be purchased by a hidden benefit."),
  reading(13, "authority", "Christian Living", "An Honest Account of What Is Owed",
    "Fulfilling lawful civic obligations without deceit", "Romans 13:7",
    "Faithfulness includes obligations that do not feel spiritually exciting. Paul places taxes, dues, respect, and honor within the ordinary conduct of believers who belong to Christ.",
    `Romans 13 speaks to Christians living under public authorities, not in a society where every ruler is righteous. Paul's instruction does not declare every government decision good. It directs believers away from dishonest evasion and toward responsible participation in civic life.

An accurate account matters. We should not invent figures, conceal income, or use a false document merely because others do so. At the same time, paying what is lawfully owed does not require submitting to extortion or abandoning lawful appeals against an improper demand. Truthfulness and justice belong together.

JCTM's concern for Christian character reaches beyond church meetings into the paperwork of daily life. Ask for competent advice where the law is unclear. Keep records that can be examined. Distinguish a legitimate concession from a concealed lie. We honor God not by presenting ourselves as exempt from common responsibilities, but by meeting those responsibilities with honesty. Our public witness includes the account nobody notices when it is done correctly, but many people suffer when it is falsified.`,
    "Review one tax, registration, or other lawful obligation. Correct an inaccurate record, seek qualified advice if needed, and refuse to use false information to obtain an advantage.",
    "Lord, help me live honestly in public as well as private. Give me wisdom to meet legitimate obligations, challenge injustice lawfully, and keep a clear conscience before You. Amen.",
    "I will give an honest account and meet my lawful obligations."),
  reading(14, "giving", "Stewardship", "Faithful When Keeping Your Word Costs",
    "Honouring an existing agreement when circumstances become inconvenient", "Psalm 15:4",
    "An agreement is easy to praise before its cost becomes clear. Psalm 15 describes integrity that does not change its word simply because faithfulness has become inconvenient.",
    `The psalm asks who may dwell in God's presence and answers with a portrait of truthful living. One mark is the person who keeps a promise even when it brings personal loss. Worship and reliability are not separate subjects.

Circumstances do sometimes make an agreement impossible or reveal that it was unjust. Honesty then requires a direct conversation, responsible renegotiation, and appropriate advice. The verse does not command participation in harm. It confronts the casual decision to abandon a lawful promise because a better opportunity has appeared.

For a Christian, another person's plans should not become disposable when our preferences change. A commitment to deliver, repay, attend, or support has shaped someone else's expectations. Consider their position before protecting only your convenience. JCTM's emphasis on stewardship is not a formula for enrichment; it is accountability for what God has entrusted to us, including our word. A dependable life is built through many decisions that may bring no applause, but allow others to trust what we say.`,
    "Review one lawful promise that now feels inconvenient. Fulfill it, or speak promptly and honestly about a necessary change. Do not leave another person carrying the consequences of your silence.",
    "Faithful God, teach me to value my word as a responsibility. Give me honesty when circumstances change and courage to bear the proper cost of keeping a promise. Amen.",
    "My word will remain trustworthy when convenience changes."),
  reading(15, "authority", "Holiness", "Respect the Boundary You Did Not Set",
    "Honouring another persons property boundaries and rightful inheritance", "Proverbs 22:28",
    "A boundary marker may seem small, yet moving it can take away another family's security. Scripture recognizes that quiet theft is still theft when it happens a little at a time.",
    `The ancient landmark identified land belonging to a household. Moving it could enlarge one person's property while erasing another person's inheritance. The warning protects those whose rights might be overcome by a stronger neighbor's ambition.

The principle reaches more than land. We can cross boundaries by taking another person's work, using resources without permission, or treating shared property as if influence made it ours. A religious purpose does not make appropriation lawful. Even a project intended for ministry must respect ownership and fair process.

This teaching also invites care where records are unclear or families disagree. Seek truthful documentation, responsible mediation, and qualified legal guidance rather than intimidation. JCTM's call to holiness includes the integrity of ordinary dealings. Refusing to move a boundary is an acknowledgment that God does not need us to gain through someone else's loss. We can pursue our responsibilities without taking possession of what was never entrusted to us. Let contentment protect the rights of the neighbor you might otherwise overlook.`,
    "Check a disputed use of property, shared funds, or another person's work. Seek permission or fair clarification. Where a boundary has been crossed, begin a responsible process of restitution.",
    "Righteous Lord, restrain my desire to enlarge myself through another person's loss. Teach me to respect rightful boundaries and to pursue peace through truthful, fair dealings. Amen.",
    "I will respect what belongs to another person."),
  reading(16, "authority", "Love", "Speak Where Silence Protects the Strong",
    "Advocating responsibly for people unable to present their own case", "Proverbs 31:8",
    "Silence is not always neutral. Sometimes it leaves the strongest voice unchallenged while a person without influence struggles to be heard.",
    `The instruction to open one's mouth for those unable to speak is given in a setting of public responsibility. It calls a ruler to use access and authority for people whose rights are easily ignored. Power is entrusted for justice, not merely personal security.

We may be able to help someone complete a complaint, explain a process, or obtain appropriate support. Advocacy does not mean inventing facts, speaking over the person, or making their suffering an opportunity for our reputation. It means taking their dignity seriously and helping truth receive a fair hearing.

JCTM's warning against oppression invites a faith that notices who is missing from the conversation. Ask whose absence makes a decision look simpler than it really is. Where abuse or danger is involved, seek trained, lawful help and protect confidentiality. Not every situation can be solved by a public speech. Sometimes the most faithful use of a voice is a careful report, a truthful witness statement, or a quiet insistence that a vulnerable person's rights must not be dismissed.`,
    "Identify someone facing a process they cannot navigate alone. Ask what support they want, help them reach a qualified advocate, and avoid sharing their story without permission.",
    "Father, give me courage to speak truthfully for the vulnerable. Keep me from careless claims or self-promotion, and make my influence useful to those who are easily ignored. Amen.",
    "I will use my voice to defend dignity and seek justice."),
  reading(17, "love", "Love", "Help the Person You Would Rather Avoid",
    "Offering practical assistance to an opponent in immediate need", "Exodus 23:5",
    "An enemy's difficulty can feel like a chance to walk away satisfied. God's law interrupts that instinct with an instruction to help lift the burden.",
    `Exodus imagines a concrete encounter: the animal of someone who hates you has collapsed beneath its load. The command moves beyond a polite attitude. The Israelite must not use hostility as permission to withhold necessary assistance.

This is a searching picture of love. We may speak warmly about loving enemies while refusing the small, practical help that would inconvenience our resentment. The passage asks whether we can recognize a real need without first requiring the other person to become agreeable.

Love does not erase prudent boundaries. Helping an opponent does not require returning to an abusive relationship, ignoring a legal restriction, or exposing ourselves to danger. Safe assistance can be arranged through others when direct contact is inappropriate. The point is to refuse malice as our guide. JCTM's emphasis on Christlike love becomes visible when another person's hardship is not treated as our entertainment. We can oppose wrongdoing and still choose mercy toward a human being whose need is in front of us.`,
    "Consider a safe, practical way to help someone with whom you disagree. Offer assistance without using it to demand reconciliation, approval, or an admission that you were right.",
    "Lord Jesus, free my compassion from the limits of personal liking. Show me how to help wisely and safely without allowing resentment to decide whose suffering matters. Amen.",
    "Disagreement will not become permission for me to withhold mercy."),
  reading(18, "love", "Holiness", "Refuse the Pleasure of Their Fall",
    "Rejecting secret delight when a rival experiences misfortune", "Proverbs 24:17",
    "A rival's failure can bring a satisfaction we would be ashamed to name publicly. Scripture addresses that hidden celebration, where resentment disguises itself as a sense of justice.",
    `The proverb does not ask us to pretend wrongdoing has no consequences. It forbids rejoicing over an enemy's fall. There is a difference between welcoming the end of harm and enjoying the humiliation of a human being.

This distinction matters when a public figure is exposed or a difficult colleague loses influence. Necessary accountability may protect others and should not be obstructed. Yet gossip, mockery, and triumphant sharing can reveal that our concern is no longer protection or truth. We are feeding a private appetite for someone else's collapse.

Christlike love allows sorrow for victims, commitment to justice, and grief over the ruin of an offender to exist together. JCTM's call to holiness must reach these secret responses, not only visible behavior. Ask what you are hoping a report will accomplish when you repeat it. Resist the idea that another person's failure proves your superiority. We all need mercy, and a clean conscience is not strengthened by celebrating the disgrace of someone we dislike.`,
    "When troubling news concerns a rival, stop before forwarding it. Share verified information only for a necessary purpose, pray for those harmed, and refuse jokes that turn suffering into entertainment.",
    "Father, expose the malice I hide beneath concern for justice. Help me welcome accountability without delighting in ruin, and keep my heart humble before Your mercy. Amen.",
    "I will seek justice without celebrating another person's disgrace."),
  reading(19, "love", "Love", "Love That Will Not Leave a Wound Untreated",
    "Addressing a friends harmful conduct instead of silently nurturing resentment", "Leviticus 19:17",
    "A smile can conceal a growing store of bitterness. Biblical love does not require pretending that a harmful pattern is harmless or allowing resentment to replace an honest conversation.",
    `Leviticus connects hatred in the heart with the responsibility to address a neighbor truthfully. Silence may appear peaceful while allowing both the offense and our inward hostility to grow. Loving correction can prevent that hidden division from becoming permanent.

The passage does not authorize constant criticism. Before speaking, examine the facts and your own motives. Choose words that identify conduct rather than condemn a person's whole identity. A clear concern is more helpful than a long accusation built from every old disappointment.

JCTM's Correction Mandate reminds us that correction is meant for the health of the body, not the pleasure of the speaker. In friendship, love may say what flattery will not. Where there is coercion, violence, or a serious power imbalance, seek appropriate protection and support instead of arranging an unsafe private confrontation. Truth and wisdom belong together. Ask God to replace silent hatred with a faithful response that seeks repentance, restored trust where possible, and the safety of those affected.`,
    "Choose one unresolved concern that has become resentment. Pray, write a specific and fair description, and seek a safe, appropriate conversation or qualified support rather than continuing indirect complaints.",
    "God of truth and love, keep me from hiding hatred behind politeness. Give me wise words, a clean motive, and courage to address harm without cruelty. Amen.",
    "My love will seek truthful healing rather than concealed resentment."),
  reading(20, "love", "Community", "Receive the Child without a Platform",
    "Valuing children who cannot advance an adults social standing", "Mark 9:37",
    "Children can become almost invisible when adults measure importance by influence. Jesus brings a child into the center of a conversation about greatness and changes the meaning of welcome.",
    `The disciples have been discussing which of them is greatest. Jesus places a child among them and speaks of receiving the child in His name. The person with little social power becomes the test of whether they understand His kingdom.

A church's care for children is therefore not a minor addition to its serious work. Patient instruction, safe environments, and respectful attention express allegiance to Christ. Children are not props for an adult's ministry image, nor interruptions to be tolerated until important people finish.

JCTM's concern for biblical Christian living calls us to honor those who cannot repay our attention with prestige. Listen when a child speaks. Take concerns about safety seriously. Support accountable safeguarding practices and never demand secrecy about harmful conduct. Welcoming a child in Jesus' name means treating their dignity as more important than an adult's convenience. A small act of attentive care may reveal more about our understanding of spiritual greatness than a public position ever could.`,
    "Give a child your unhurried attention today, with appropriate boundaries. Support one practical safeguarding or teaching responsibility, and make sure children know which trusted adults can help when something feels wrong.",
    "Lord Jesus, teach me to welcome children with patience, respect, and protective care. Keep me from measuring people by influence, and make our fellowship safe for the young. Amen.",
    "Children entrusted to our care will be treated with dignity and safety."),
  reading(21, "primitive", "Christian Living", "Let the Journey Become a Lesson",
    "Teaching children Gods Word through ordinary daily conversations", "Deuteronomy 6:7",
    "Faith is not learned only in a formal lesson. A journey, a meal, or a question at bedtime can become a place where a child sees how God's Word touches life.",
    `Deuteronomy places the instruction of children within the rhythms of a household. Parents are to speak of God's commands while sitting, walking, lying down, and rising. The picture is repeated, patient conversation rather than a single impressive speech.

This does not mean turning every moment into a sermon or silencing a child's questions. It means making truth intelligible through ordinary situations. An apology can explain repentance. A fair decision can illustrate justice. A prayer before a difficult appointment can show dependence on God.

JCTM's return to biblical Christianity should be visible in the way adults hand faith to the next generation. Children notice the difference between what we teach and what we practice. When we fail, an honest confession can teach more than a performance of faultlessness. Use age-appropriate words and allow questions without shame. A faithful household is not one in which every conversation sounds religious, but one in which God's truth has become a trustworthy guide to the ordinary business of living together.`,
    "Choose one everyday event to discuss with a child in your care. Connect it to a short Bible passage, invite their questions, and model the response you are asking them to learn.",
    "Father, make me patient in passing on Your Word. Help my example support my instruction, and teach me to answer young minds with honesty, warmth, and humility. Amen.",
    "I will make ordinary moments opportunities for faithful instruction."),
  reading(22, "love", "Patience", "Discipline without Crushing the Spirit",
    "Correcting children without provoking them through harshness or inconsistency", "Ephesians 6:4",
    "Correction can train a child, but repeated humiliation can teach only fear. Paul gives fathers responsibility not merely for enforcing rules, but for the spirit in which instruction is given.",
    `Ephesians speaks of bringing children up in the nurture and admonition of the Lord while refusing provocation. Parental authority is real, yet it remains accountable to Christ. A child's dependence does not give an adult permission to be cruel.

Provocation can come through unpredictable punishments, public shaming, impossible expectations, or a refusal to listen. Biblical discipline aims at understanding and growth, not the release of an adult's anger. Clear boundaries and proportionate consequences are strongest when accompanied by patience and a consistent example.

JCTM's emphasis on holiness must include the treatment of those with the least power at home. Ask whether a child learns truth from your correction or simply learns that the strongest person wins. Apologize when you have acted wrongly. Seek qualified support where anger feels unmanageable or harm has occurred. God's instruction is not a cover for abuse. A home shaped by Christ can be firm about wrongdoing while protecting a child's dignity and growing capacity to understand.`,
    "Review a recent correction. Replace one harsh or inconsistent habit with a clear expectation, calm explanation, and age-appropriate consequence. If you have harmed or frightened a child, seek accountable help.",
    "Lord, govern my anger before I correct another person. Help me guide children with patience and consistency, and never use Your name to justify humiliating or harming them. Amen.",
    "My correction will seek a child's growth, not the relief of my anger."),
  reading(23, "love", "Love", "Honor Where Familiarity Has Grown",
    "Treating a spouse with understanding and equal spiritual dignity", "1 Peter 3:7",
    "Familiarity can make courtesy disappear at home. Peter addresses husbands with a command to live with understanding and honor, reminding them that a wife shares in the grace of life.",
    `The instruction joins marriage conduct to prayer. A husband cannot treat his wife with contempt and imagine that his religious life remains untouched. She is not an accessory to his calling but a fellow heir of God's gracious gift.

Understanding requires attention. It asks how a spouse experiences our words, decisions, and expectations. Honor refuses ridicule, coercion, and the use of spiritual authority to silence a legitimate concern. Mutual dignity is not weakened when practical responsibilities differ; it is the condition under which those responsibilities should be carried.

JCTM's teaching on transformed Christian living reaches the private conversations that visitors never hear. A public reputation for devotion is not a substitute for respect at home. Where a marriage includes violence or coercive control, safety and qualified support must take priority; this passage must never be used to require someone to endure harm. Today, consider whether the person closest to you experiences your faith as patient understanding or merely as a demand to accommodate your preferences.`,
    "Ask your spouse one sincere question about a concern you have overlooked. Listen without interruption, agree on a practical response, and seek responsible counseling where the pattern requires more than one conversation.",
    "Father, remove contempt and selfishness from my closest relationships. Teach me to honor my spouse with understanding, truthful speech, and practical care worthy of a fellow heir of grace. Amen.",
    "Familiarity will not become an excuse for dishonor in my home."),
  reading(24, "love", "Christian Living", "Answer the Heat without Adding Fire",
    "Deescalating an angry exchange through a gentle response", "Proverbs 15:1",
    "An angry voice invites an angry answer, and soon the original issue disappears beneath the struggle to win. Wisdom offers a response that lowers the heat without abandoning truth.",
    `A soft answer turns away wrath, but harsh words stir anger. This is a description of wise conduct, not a promise that every angry person will immediately become safe. The responsibility given to us is the manner of our answer, not control over another person's reaction.

Gentleness may mean lowering our voice, naming the issue simply, or asking to continue when both people can speak responsibly. It does not mean agreeing with a lie or remaining in a dangerous exchange. We can set a clear boundary without using contempt.

The love of Christ must shape not only the message we defend but the tone in which we defend it. At home, in traffic, or online, a quick insult can create a wound much larger than the disagreement. JCTM's concern for Christian character invites us to notice these ordinary tests. Before answering, ask whether your next sentence will clarify the problem or merely punish the speaker. Strength under God's control can be heard in a voice that refuses unnecessary fire.`,
    "Prepare one calm sentence for a recurring conflict: name the concern and propose a respectful next step. If the exchange becomes threatening, leave safely and seek appropriate support rather than escalating it.",
    "Lord, place a guard over my first response to anger. Give me words that are truthful and gentle, and wisdom to set boundaries without returning contempt for contempt. Amen.",
    "I will answer with controlled strength rather than unnecessary anger."),
  reading(25, "holiness", "Love", "Cultivate the Covenant You Already Have",
    "Nurturing marital faithfulness rather than entertaining romantic alternatives", "Proverbs 5:18",
    "Neglect can make a distant fantasy look more attractive than a real relationship. Proverbs directs a married person's attention back to the covenant already entrusted to them.",
    `Proverbs 5 warns against adultery and its destructive consequences. Its positive instruction is not merely to avoid a forbidden relationship, but to find joy within marriage. Faithfulness includes the cultivation of affection, attention, and gratitude.

This challenges the private comparisons that make a spouse seem ordinary while an outsider appears effortless. A fantasy has no household responsibilities, disagreements, or history of fatigue. It is unfair to measure a real person against an edited possibility. Secrecy and emotional investment outside the covenant should be addressed before they grow into betrayal.

JCTM's emphasis on moral purity invites practical, not performative, boundaries. Protect appropriate communication, seek honest counsel, and invest time in the relationship you have promised to honor. Where harm or abuse exists, fidelity must not be used to obstruct safety or qualified support. The passage calls us away from divided desire, not toward the concealment of suffering. Let holiness include the patient work of cherishing rather than treating commitment as a restriction on personal excitement.`,
    "End one secretive or inappropriate pattern of communication. Arrange an intentional time with your spouse, and seek trustworthy counseling if distance, betrayal, or serious conflict requires accountable help.",
    "Faithful God, guard my desires and strengthen my integrity. Help me honor my marriage covenant through truthful boundaries, patient affection, and the willingness to do necessary relational work. Amen.",
    "I will protect my covenant from divided and secretive affection."),
  reading(26, "love", "Community", "Care Begins Close to Home",
    "Taking practical responsibility for ageing parents and dependent relatives", "1 Timothy 5:4",
    "It can feel easier to support a public cause than to keep showing up for an ageing relative. Paul brings ordinary family care into the meaning of godliness.",
    `In a discussion about support for widows, Paul directs children and grandchildren to practice responsibility within their own household. Care is not left entirely to an institution when family members can appropriately contribute. Gratitude for earlier care should become practical provision.

Family situations differ. Some relatives are estranged, some caregivers have limited resources, and some relationships include a history of harm. The instruction does not demand unsafe contact or impossible self-exhaustion. It calls us to ask honestly what responsible help is available rather than using difficulty as a reason to disappear.

JCTM's concern for Christian living includes medicine collected, appointments arranged, and patient attention given to a repeated story. Sharing responsibilities can prevent one caregiver from carrying an invisible burden alone. Discuss needs clearly and seek community or professional support where necessary. Godliness is not measured only by public activity. A quiet act of care for someone whose strength is diminishing may be one of the most direct ways we honor the faith we profess.`,
    "Contact a dependent or ageing relative, where safe. Identify one concrete need and share a realistic care plan with other responsible family members rather than leaving the whole burden to one person.",
    "Father, make my gratitude practical toward those who need care. Give our family wisdom, fair responsibility, and compassion, and sustain those carrying long and difficult caregiving duties. Amen.",
    "My faith will include responsible care for vulnerable family members."),
  reading(27, "primitive", "Gratitude", "Tell Them What God Has Done",
    "Passing truthful accounts of Gods faithfulness to the next generation", "Psalm 78:4",
    "A family's stories can teach children what deserves admiration. Psalm 78 calls God's people to pass on His works, so that the next generation learns where hope belongs.",
    `The psalm is not a collection of flattering family memories. It recounts God's faithfulness alongside human stubbornness. The purpose is that children should set their hope in God and not repeat the disobedience of earlier generations.

Our testimony should have the same honesty. We need not embellish an event, invent a miracle, or hide every failure to make God seem faithful. A truthful account of provision, correction, or grace after repentance can help a young person understand dependence on the Lord.

JCTM's emphasis on returning to the faith of the apostles includes handing on a trustworthy witness rather than spiritual folklore. Tell what you know, distinguish observation from interpretation, and allow questions. A story becomes more useful when it points beyond the storyteller. Ask what your children or younger believers are learning from the accounts you repeat. Are they hearing only about human success, or about the God whose mercy and truth have carried a people who often needed correction?`,
    "Share one accurate account of God's faithfulness with a younger person. Include what you learned and any mistake you had to acknowledge. Avoid exaggeration, and read a related passage together.",
    "Lord, make my testimony truthful and useful. Help me pass on gratitude rather than self-importance, and give the next generation a steady reason to place their hope in You. Amen.",
    "I will tell God's faithfulness truthfully to those who come after me."),
  reading(28, "love", "Christian Living", "Do Not Teach a Household to Compete",
    "Recognizing and refusing destructive favouritism among children", "Genesis 37:4",
    "An unequal pattern of affection can speak long before anyone names it. Joseph's brothers recognized their father's preference, and the household became a place where resentment learned to grow.",
    `Genesis does not excuse the brothers' cruelty, but it shows the damaged family setting in which jealousy developed. Jacob's visible favoritism did not create innocence in those who later did wrong. It did contribute to a household where brothers struggled to speak peaceably.

Parents and caregivers must therefore examine not only whether they love each child, but how their conduct communicates that love. Different children need different support, and fairness is not always identical treatment. The danger is making one child the permanent measure by which the others feel lesser.

Christlike love refuses to make affection a prize for similarity, achievement, or usefulness. JCTM's call to practical holiness asks adults to correct patterns they may have inherited without considering their effect. Listen to a child's experience without dismissing it as jealousy. Acknowledge where comparison has wounded. Wise love can celebrate distinct gifts without creating a hierarchy of worth. The work of peace sometimes begins when an adult stops asking children to compete for assurance that they belong.`,
    "Review the comparisons you make among children or younger relatives. Replace a ranking statement with specific encouragement for each person, and listen seriously if someone describes a pattern of exclusion.",
    "Father, reveal favoritism I have normalized. Help me love each child with fairness and understanding, and repair the wounds created by careless comparison or unequal attention. Amen.",
    "I will encourage distinct gifts without ranking human worth."),
  reading(29, "love", "Community", "Let the Work Become Shared",
    "Building cooperative responsibility instead of isolated effort in a household", "Ecclesiastes 4:9",
    "A home can contain several people while one person carries almost every practical burden. Ecclesiastes invites us to see the value of companionship that becomes shared labor.",
    `The passage considers the vulnerability of isolation. Two can help one another in work, difficulty, and danger. The point is not that marriage solves every problem or that single people lack a full life. It is that human strength is not designed to operate without mutual support.

In a household, partnership becomes visible through ordinary tasks. Planning, earning, cleaning, caring, and remembering appointments all require effort. Work that is unseen is still work. Assuming that one person will carry it indefinitely can turn companionship into a source of exhaustion.

JCTM's teaching on Christlike love calls us away from entitlement toward responsible cooperation. Speak openly about capacity, health, time, and skills. A fair arrangement may change as circumstances change. The aim is not a competition over who suffers more, but a practical expression of belonging to one another. Ask which burden has become invisible because somebody faithful has carried it quietly for so long. Sharing it may be a more meaningful declaration of love than another appreciative speech.`,
    "List the visible and invisible work needed in your household. Agree on one fair redistribution, considering each person's capacity, and set a date to review whether the arrangement is actually helping.",
    "Lord, teach us to notice one another's labor. Replace entitlement with cooperation, and help our home become a place where burdens are shared with fairness and love. Amen.",
    "I will contribute to the shared work of the people entrusted to me."),
  reading(30, "primitive", "Faith", "A House Needs More Than Builders",
    "Depending on God rather than treating household security as selfmade", "Psalm 127:1",
    "We can plan carefully and still discover how little we control. Psalm 127 does not despise effort; it teaches builders and watchmen to recognize the Lord on whom their work depends.",
    `The psalm places building, guarding, labor, and family life beneath God's care. Human effort becomes vain when it imagines itself sufficient apart from Him. The answer is not laziness but a different foundation for responsibility.

A household needs budgets, maintenance, honest work, and wise boundaries. Prayer is not a substitute for these duties. Yet no arrangement can guarantee a life untouched by loss or uncertainty. Dependence on God frees us from treating control as the condition of peace.

JCTM's biblical emphasis resists the idea that a religious formula can purchase an invulnerable home. We ask the Lord to establish what is faithful and correct what is built on pride. We do our work and entrust what lies beyond us to His wisdom. Invite Him into the decisions about money, relationships, and priorities that define the house from within. A secure-looking building is not the same as a household grounded in truth, love, and a humble acknowledgment that our lives are received rather than self-created.`,
    "Bring one household plan before God with the people it affects. Identify your practical responsibility and the outcome you cannot control. Take the responsible step without pretending you can guarantee the result.",
    "Father, establish our home in truth and love. Bless honest labor, correct proud plans, and teach us to depend on You without neglecting the responsibilities You have given us. Amen.",
    "I will labor faithfully and entrust my household to the Lord."),
];
